Lyne is a casual puzzle game created by Thomas Bowker in March 2014. With hundreds of pre-built jigsaw puzzles, your goal is to draw a continuous line that joins together shapes of the same type.
It has a minimalist design and a great soundtrack that will make you feel at peace while playing the game. However, later levels will give you some headaches due to the connections, which will require you to resolve the level with multiple lines, shapes and colors.
If you are looking for a peaceful puzzle game to play, you need to check this out.
- Hundreds of puzzles;
- Excellent visual and music;
- After a while, puzzles can become repetitive;

What's great about this game is the complexity of it's various systems : different skills allowing for specialisation and great multiplayer experiences, various crafting from some unusual materials (ex.: a pipe bomb, some proximity trigger from alarm systems, etc).  All of this set in a post apocalyptic world where every ressources count.  Even running water and electricity will eventually die down (from instant to up to 12 months, depending on the settings).
There are many ways you can die : zombies, starvation/thirst, or even just being dumb about building and falling to your death.  But there as much ways to survive, at least for a while.
Don't like the hardcore stuff (I know I don't), you can tweak the settings very easily for single player maps or even in multiplayer maps (where you're admin).
Don't be deceived by the retro look... this game has a lot of computing going on in the background.  Saves games (assuming your IWBUMS-ing) can go into the 100mb per save game! (depending on how much of the map you've explored)
If you alt-tab out of your game while in fullscreen, you may not always be able to go back in.  Whatever you do, don't close the game or you'll loose some items due to the map and character not saving in synch.  Items or progress on the map will go missing and your character won't have them as well (so not exploitable either).  What you need to do, is to :
1) open your task manager ;
2) open the ProjectZomboid32.exe file tree (wait... wasn't there an option for 64 bits?) ;
3) right click on Project Zomboid ;
4) select "Switch to".
That works every time without any item loss.  Thank you Easy Pickins :)

